### Logical Relationships Between Goals and Tasks

| **Parent Goal** | **Children** | **Relation** | **Runtime** | **Justification** |
|-----------------|--------------|--------------|-------------|-------------------|
| G1 | G2, G3 | AND | `;` | The operation must first acquire the meal(s) (G2) and then deliver them (G3). |
| G2 | AT1 | AND | – | The robot must pick up the meal; no other subtasks are required. |
| G3 | G4a, G4b | OR (fallback) | `;` | Either delivery method can satisfy the goal; if manipulation fails, tray retrieval is used. |
| G4a | AT2 | AND | – | Manipulation of the meal onto the table is the only required action. |
| G4b | G5, G6, G7, G8, G9, G10, G11, G12 | AND | `;` | All subtasks must be executed in order to complete tray‑based delivery and related housekeeping tasks. |
| G5 | AT3 | AND | – | The robot queries the patient record to assess capability. |
| G6 | AT4 | AND | – | Retrieval requires cooperation; the task captures this requirement. |
| G7 | AT5 | AND | – | The robot indicates the meal to the patient. |
| G8 | AT6 | AND | – | Tracking is performed by the robot. |
| G9 | AT7 | AND | – | Alerting wrong meals is a direct action. |
| G10 | AT8 | AND | – | Dish retrieval is a distinct task. |
| G11 | AT9 | AND | – | Door opening is an independent action. |
| G12 | AT10 | AND | – | The robot responds to the call and performs dish retrieval. |

---

**Summary**

The goal model above captures the entire food‑delivery mission as a hierarchical tree of intentional goals (Perform, Query, Achieve) decomposed into concrete tasks that can be executed by one or multiple robots. Each node’s runtime operator and logical relation has been chosen to reflect the mission description: sequential steps where order matters, parallel execution where independent actions can occur together, and fallback/OR choices where multiple methods are available. The tables provide a clear, justifiable mapping from mission intent to actionable robot behavior.
